# ---------------- the whole dog ----------------
N('dog', 'Dog (Labrador retriever type)', 'organism', .57, ['bio_mammals_primates'],
  'The domestic dog (Canis familiaris) in a Labrador-retriever shape: a 30 kg working gundog that hunts with its nose, hears sounds people cannot, and runs on its toes.',
  'Click any part of the dog to meet it. The layer buttons peel it like the human body: coat, muscles, skeleton, organs, nerves, and heart and vessels. Almost every bone and organ has a counterpart in you, so each card says what is the same and what differs. The "Inside" buttons open the heart, the eye, the nose and the ear.',
  'A dog is a mammal, so it has the same plan as you: a backbone, four limbs, a four-chambered heart, lungs, a liver and a brain. The differences come from how it lives. It walks on its toes (digitigrade), so its heel is the "hock" halfway up the hind leg. It has a long nose lined with far more smell receptors than yours, big movable ears, and two kinds of colour-sensing cells in the eye instead of three. It cools itself by panting, not by sweating.',
  'The model is a Labrador-type male, 57 cm tall at the withers (the top of the shoulders) and 30 kg, inside the breed-standard ranges. Breed standards give height and weight for a type of dog, not for every dog, and dogs vary widely in size. A healthy dog\'s resting heart rate is about 70-120 beats a minute and its normal body temperature is about 37.5-39.2 C.',
  [['Height at withers (model)', '57 cm (UK Kennel Club: dogs 56-57 cm; AKC: 57-62 cm)'], ['Weight (model)', '30 kg (AKC: males 29-36 kg)'], ['Teeth', '42 (people: 32)'], ['Vertebrae', '7 neck, 13 chest, 7 lower back, 3 fused sacral, about 20 tail'], ['Rib pairs', '13 (people: 12)'], ['Resting heart rate', '≈ 70-120 per minute'], ['Body temperature', '≈ 37.5-39.2 C'], ['Typical lifespan, Labradors', 'median ≈ 12 years (UK study)']],
  refs=[R('29.6', '29-6-mammals'), R('27.2', '27-2-features-used-to-classify-animals')], ext=[UKC, AKC, MERCK_T, MERCK_HR, LIFE], human=[('human_body', 'Compare with the whole human body in the same park: the two skeletons share nearly every bone.')],
  parts=['dog_coat_group', 'dog_skeleton', 'dog_muscles', 'dog_organs', 'dog_spinal_cord', 'dog_arteries', 'dog_heart', 'dog_eye'],
  dyk='A dog\'s ancestor was a wolf. People and dogs have lived together for at least about 15,000 years, so a dog\'s body has been shaped as much by us as by nature.',
  openq=['When, where and how many times were dogs first domesticated? Genetic and archaeological evidence disagree.'], cert='C1')
blocks('dog', [('w', 'Wolf-like ancestor', 15, 20, None), ('d', 'Domestic dog', 50, 50, 'green'), ('b', 'Breeds: from 2 kg to over 70 kg', 85, 80, 'orange'), ('l', 'Labrador: gundog, 30 kg', 85, 20, None)], [('w', 'd', 'domestication'), ('d', 'b', 'selective breeding'), ('d', 'l', None)], 'One species, a huge range of sizes. The model stands for a medium, athletic breed; the body plan is the same in all.', False)
N('dog_coat_group', 'The dog\'s outside', 'structure', .6, ['dog'], 'Skin, fur, ears, nose, eyes, claws and pads: everything you can see on the outside of the dog.',
  'The coat button shows the dog as you would meet it. Click the fur, an ear, the nose, a paw pad or a claw.', 'The skin is a waterproof, living wrapper made of layers; hair grows out of it, and the nose, pads and claws are special skin.', 'Together they handle touch, temperature, protection and scent.',
  [['Parts', 'skin and coat, fur, ears, nose, eyes, paw pads, claws']], parts=['dog_coat', 'dog_fur', 'dog_ear', 'dog_nose', 'dog_eye', 'dog_pads', 'dog_claws'])
N('dog_skeleton', 'The dog\'s skeleton', 'structure', .6, ['dog'],
  'A dog has about 320 bones, built on the same plan as yours but shaped for running on four legs: a long spine, 13 pairs of ribs, a shoulder blade on the side of the chest, and toes that bear the weight.',
  'The skeleton holds the dog up, protects the brain, heart and lungs, and gives the muscles something to pull on. Press "Skeleton" and click a bone. Nearly every bone has a human counterpart, and the cards say which.',
  'Compared with a person: the dog has no working collarbone, so the front legs are held to the body by muscle and the shoulder blade can slide along the side of the chest, lengthening the stride. The dog walks on its toes, so its wrist (carpus) and ankle (tarsus, the hock) are halfway up the leg, not at the ground. It has more vertebrae in the chest and a long tail.',
  'Counts differ a little between dogs: the tail vertebrae are about 20 but may be 19-23, and some dogs have a transitional extra lumbar vertebra. A commonly quoted total of about 320 bones depends on how many tail bones are counted and on small bones such as sesamoids.',
  [['Bones in total', '≈ 320 (varies with tail length)'], ['Vertebrae', 'C7 T13 L7 S3 Cd≈20'], ['Teeth', '42'], ['Collarbone', 'none that joins the shoulder (a vestige in muscle)']], cert='C2',
  refs=[SK, R('38.1', '38-1-types-of-skeletal-systems')], ext=[WDS, VERT, WDA], human=[('skeletal_system', 'In people: 206 bones; compare the human skeleton.')], parts=['dog_skull', 'dog_cervical', 'dog_ribs', 'dog_pelvis', 'dog_scapula', 'dog_femur'])
bars('dog_skeleton', [('dog: thoracic vertebrae', 13), ('human: thoracic vertebrae', 12), ('dog: lumbar vertebrae', 7), ('human: lumbar vertebrae', 5), ('dog: rib pairs', 13), ('human: rib pairs', 12)], 'count', 'Dog and human side by side. The dog figures are from the vertebral formula in the open-access paper cited on this card and the 13 rib pairs on the Ribs card; the human figures are on the human cards linked above. The cervical count is 7 in both.')
N('dog_muscles', 'The dog\'s muscles', 'structure', .6, ['dog'],
  'Muscle is most of what a dog is made of by mass: sheets and spindles of fibre that pull on bones, with the biggest ones in the shoulder, back and haunch that drive running.',
  'Press "Muscles" and click a muscle. The biggest muscles are in the hind legs (they push the dog forward) and along the back (they flex and straighten the spine at a gallop).',
  'Muscles are named by position and job, mostly with the same Latin names as in people. The hind legs provide most of the push, and the shoulder and neck muscles steer and carry the head. Tendons (the shiny cords) join muscle to bone and store elastic energy.',
  'In this model the muscles fill the space between the skeleton and the skin. Their outlines are schematic: each muscle is drawn as the region of the body nearest to a core line that marks where that muscle runs, so their boundaries are approximate and many deeper muscles are not drawn as separate parts.',
  [['Muscle shown', '31 groups, left and right'], ['Skeletal muscle share of body mass', 'not stated here: published figures were not checked']], cert='C2',
  refs=[A('10.2', '10-2-skeletal-muscle'), R('38.4', '38-4-muscle-contraction-and-locomotion')], ext=[WDA, UMN], human=[('muscular_system', 'In people, see the muscular system; most dog muscles are the same named muscles.')], parts=['dog_m_triceps', 'dog_m_quadriceps', 'dog_m_masseter'])
N('dog_organs', 'The dog\'s organs', 'structure', .6, ['dog'], 'Brain, tongue, windpipe, lungs, heart, diaphragm, liver, stomach, spleen, intestines, kidneys and bladder, in the same arrangement as in a person but lying along a horizontal body.',
  'Press "Organs". The chest holds the heart and lungs, a muscular sheet (the diaphragm) separates it from the belly, and the belly holds the digestive organs.',
  'A dog\'s organs are the same set as yours. The main difference is posture: they hang from a horizontal spine, not stacked on a vertical one, and the intestines are short for an animal that eats meat.',
  'The positions here are typical, not measured on one animal, and the intestines are drawn as a schematic coil.', [['Organs shown', 'brain to bladder, 19 parts']], cert='C2', refs=[R('33.1', '33-1-animal-form-and-function')], ext=[WDA],
  human=[('human_body', 'Compare the human organs in the body in this park.')], parts=['dog_brain', 'dog_lungs', 'dog_heart', 'dog_liver', 'dog_stomach', 'dog_kidneys'])

# ---------------- coat ----------------
N('dog_coat', 'Skin and coat', 'structure', .005, ['dog_coat_group'],
  'A dog\'s skin is a thin, tough layer topped by a double coat: stiff guard hairs over a soft undercoat, which together keep it warm and dry.',
  'The skin keeps water in and germs out, senses touch, and grows hair. A Labrador\'s coat is short and dense and sheds water, which is why the breed can swim in cold water for long periods. Press the button to look inside the skin.',
  'Dog hairs grow in groups: one large guard hair (primary hair) and a tuft of finer hairs (secondary hairs) share one opening at the skin surface, called a compound follicle; about 3-15 smaller hairs may join one central hair. The undercoat traps air for warmth, the guard hairs shed water. Hair follows a growth cycle (growing, regressing, resting), and dogs shed mostly in early spring and early autumn. Sweat glands of the usual (merocrine) kind exist on the paw pads only; the glands that come with the hair follicles are of the scent-producing (apocrine) kind, so dogs do not cool by sweating over the body.',
  'The outer layer of the epidermis, the stratum corneum, is a barrier of dead flattened cells held together by lipids (ceramides, cholesterol and free fatty acids). Colour comes from melanin in the hair shaft; Labrador colours are black, yellow (cream to fox red) and chocolate. This model is yellow. The fur drawn on the model is a sample of short strokes, a small fraction of the real number of hairs.',
  [['Coat', 'short, dense, water-resistant double coat (breed standard)'], ['Hairs per compound follicle', '≈ 3-15 smaller hairs with one central hair'], ['Shedding', 'mainly early spring and autumn'], ['Sweat glands', 'merocrine only on the paw pads']],
  refs=[A('5.1', '5-1-layers-of-the-skin'), A('5.2', '5-2-accessory-structures-of-the-skin')], ext=[UKC, AKC, MERCK_SKIN, SCLIP],
  human=[('skin', 'In people: the same layers (epidermis, dermis, hypodermis). Differences: people are almost hairless and sweat from the whole skin, dogs are furred and pant.'), ('hair_follicle', 'Compare the human hair follicle.'), ('sweat_gland', 'Compare human sweat glands.')],
  parts=['dog_fur', 'dog_skin_epidermis', 'dog_follicle', 'dog_undercoat'], openq=['Why did domestic dogs end up with so many coat types (short, curly, long, wire)? Several genes are known, but the full picture is still being worked out.'], cert='C1')
N('dog_fur', 'Fur', 'structure', .015, ['dog_coat'],
  'The fur is the visible hair of the dog: long, stiff guard hairs on top of a dense, soft undercoat.',
  'Each hair is a thin thread of dead cells packed with a tough protein called keratin. Guard hairs lie flat in the direction the dog is facing and shed rain; the undercoat underneath traps a layer of warm air.',
  'A hair grows from a follicle in the skin. Only the root is alive. As the cells push up, they fill with keratin, die, and form the shaft. The coat is shed and renewed in a cycle. The lean of the hairs from head to tail is why a dog\'s coat feels smooth stroked one way and rough the other.',
  'Keratin is a family of fibrous proteins that form bundles called intermediate filaments, held together by disulfide bonds. The same proteins make claws and the outer skin. This card cannot link to a keratin molecule because the explorer does not yet include one.',
  [['Model strokes drawn', '≈ 9,000 (a real coat has far more hairs)'], ['Hair type', 'guard hairs over undercoat']], cert='C1', refs=[A('5.2', '5-2-accessory-structures-of-the-skin')], ext=[MERCK_SKIN],
  human=[('hair_follicle', 'In people: the same kind of hair, but finer and sparser on most of the body.')])
N('dog_ear', 'Ears (pinnae)', 'structure', .11, ['dog_coat_group'],
  'The outer ear of a Labrador hangs down, but the same flap of cartilage can be raised and turned in many other dogs, to catch sound.',
  'The ear flap, or pinna, gathers sound and funnels it into the ear canal. A dog can move its ears independently, which helps it find where a sound comes from. Press "Inside the ear" to see the canal and inner ear.',
  'Eighteen or more muscles move a dog\'s ear (according to a standard reference), so a dog with prick ears can swivel them toward a sound. Dogs hear a wider range than people: about 67 Hz to about 45 kHz against about 20 Hz to 20 kHz for people. Floppy ears like the Labrador\'s are a trait selected during domestication; the cartilage of the flap is the same.',
  'The pinna is elastic cartilage covered in skin and hair. Sound waves are funnelled into the vertical and then horizontal parts of the ear canal to the eardrum. Floppy ears may reduce hearing slightly in some conditions; how much is still being measured.',
  [['Hearing range, dog', '≈ 67 Hz to 45 kHz'], ['Hearing range, human', '≈ 20 Hz to 20 kHz'], ['Ear muscles', '18 or more']], cert='C2', refs=[R('36.4', '36-4-hearing-and-vestibular-sensation')], ext=[WHEAR, WDA],
  human=[('human_body', 'In people the outer ear is a smaller, fixed flap; few people can move it.')], parts=['dog_ear_canal', 'dog_cochlea'])
bars('dog_ear', [('dog: lowest sound (Hz)', 67), ('human: lowest sound (Hz)', 20), ('dog: highest sound (Hz)', 45000), ('human: highest sound (Hz)', 20000)], 'frequency (Hz)', 'Range of hearing from the Hearing range reference. Dogs hear much higher sounds than people but lose out at the very low end.', logy=True)
N('dog_nose', 'Nose (rhinarium)', 'structure', .04, ['dog_coat_group'], 'The bare, moist, dark tip of the nose, with a slit on each side, is where air and scent first enter.',
  'The tip of a dog\'s nose is bare skin called the rhinarium. It is usually moist and cool, and each nostril has a slit that helps shape the air into a flow that goes to smell. Press "Inside the nose" to see where the air goes.',
  'The rhinarium is hairless, linked by a groove (the philtrum) to the upper lip, and connected through ducts to the vomeronasal organ (the organ for chemical signals). It is moist in healthy dogs and has cold-sensitive nerve endings that may help a dog sense which way the wind blows; the exact role of the wetness is still unsettled.',
  'The nose pad is black on a yellow Labrador in the breed standard, and it can be brown in chocolate dogs. Air is sniffed in short sniffs; part of it goes to the olfactory region at the back of the nasal cavity rather than straight to the lungs.',
  [['Skin type', 'hairless, moist rhinarium'], ['Role of moisture', 'not settled']], cert='C2', ext=[WRHIN, UKC], human=[('nose_airway', 'In people: a nose with no bare moist tip; compare the human nasal airway.')], parts=['dog_turb_olf', 'dog_turb_resp'],
  openq=['Why is a healthy dog\'s nose wet? Several ideas exist (catching scent molecules, cooling, sensing wind) and none has settled the question.'])
N('dog_pads', 'Paw pads', 'structure', .03, ['dog_coat_group'], 'Each foot has a thick, hairless cushion under every toe and a big one under the middle of the foot.',
  'The pads are shock absorbers and grippers. A dog walks on its toes, so the pads under the toe tips and the larger pad under the palm bear the weight.',
  'Pads are thick, tough skin over fat and connective tissue. They are the only place a dog has the ordinary kind of sweat gland (merocrine), so wet pawprints on a hot day or when stressed are sweat. The paws also carry a pad at the back of the wrist on the forelimb (the carpal pad).',
  'The pad surface is a thick keratinised epidermis. The paws are a route for losing a little heat, but panting does most of the cooling.', [['Pads per forefoot', '4 toe pads, 1 palm pad, 1 wrist pad']], cert='C2', ext=[MERCK_SKIN],
  human=[('epidermis', 'In people the soles also have thick skin and sweat glands, but people walk on the heel and whole foot.')])
N('dog_claws', 'Claws', 'structure', .02, ['dog_coat_group'], 'A claw is a curved horn sheath that grows over the last toe bone; dogs cannot pull them back.',
  'Dogs use claws for grip and digging. They are blunt compared with a cat\'s because they are always out and wear down on the ground.',
  'The claw grows from the last toe bone (the third phalanx) and is made of keratin, the same protein as hair and the outer skin. It grows continuously, so it must wear down or be trimmed. The dewclaw (the first toe) on the inner side of the foreleg often does not touch the ground.',
  'The living core of the claw (the quick) has blood vessels and nerves.', [['Material', 'keratin']], cert='C1', human=[('hand_bones_l', 'People have flat nails on the same last toe bones.')])
N('dog_eye', 'Eye', 'organ', .022, ['dog_coat_group'], 'The dog\'s eyeball is about 2 cm across, and it works like yours but has a mirror layer behind the retina that gives dogs better vision in dim light.',
  'The eye is a ball of clear parts that focus light onto the retina, which turns it into signals for the brain. Press "Inside the eye" to cut it open and see the layers.',
  'The eye is the same design as the human eye: cornea, iris, lens, vitreous gel, retina. Differences: a dog has a reflective layer, the tapetum lucidum, behind the retina; a third eyelid (the nictitating membrane) that slides across the eye; only two kinds of colour-sensing cones instead of three; and a much larger set of rod cells for dim light. A dog\'s sharpness (acuity) is lower than a person\'s, about 20/75 in a common estimate.',
  'A dog\'s eyes face forward, but less so than ours, so the field of view is wide with a smaller region seen by both eyes (the numbers were not checked here). The third eyelid\'s gland can swell out of place in some dogs ("cherry eye").',
  [['Eyeball width', '≈ 2 cm (a general reference, medium dog; not a breed measurement)'], ['Cone types', '2 (peaks ≈ 429 and ≈ 555 nm)'], ['Visual acuity', '≈ 20/75 (estimate)']], cert='C2',
  refs=[R('36.5', '36-5-vision')], ext=[DVMEYE, BEAGLE, WNICT], human=[('eye_l', 'In people: the same parts; people have three cone types, a fovea, and no tapetum.'), ('retina', 'Compare the human retina.')],
  parts=['dog_tapetum', 'dog_retina', 'dog_lens', 'dog_cornea', 'dog_iris', 'dog_sclera', 'dog_choroid', 'dog_vitreous'])

# ---------------- skeleton parts ----------------
def B(id, title, scale, summary, basics, deeper, expert, facts, human, parents=None, cert='C1', refs=None, ext=None, openq=None, parts=None, dyk=None):
    N(id, title, 'structure', scale, parents or ['dog_skeleton'], summary, basics, deeper, expert, facts, cert=cert, refs=refs or [SK], ext=ext or [WDS], human=human, parts=parts, openq=openq, dyk=dyk)
B('dog_skull', 'Skull', .2, 'The dog\'s skull has a long muzzle, a braincase at the back, and a bony arch on each side for the big chewing muscles.',
  'The skull protects the brain and holds the eyes, the nose passages and the teeth. A Labrador has a broad skull with a moderately long muzzle. The long muzzle contains the nasal cavity, where the scent-sensing lining sits. Press "Inside the nose" to look into it.',
  'Same bones as a person\'s skull, arranged differently: the face (the muzzle) is long and sticks forward, while ours is flat and tucked under the braincase. A bony arch on each side (the zygomatic arch) leaves room for the jaw muscles, and the eye sockets are open at the side of the head. Skull shapes vary more between dog breeds than between almost any other animals.',
  'In the model the skull is a single solid with a hollow braincase and a nasal cavity, so a cut shows them. The sutures between the skull bones and the finer sinuses are not drawn.',
  [['Skull length', '≈ 20 cm in this model (modelled from proportions)'], ['Teeth', '42']],
  [('skull', 'In people: the skull, 22 bones; same bones, but the human face is short and flat.')], refs=[A('7.2', '7-2-the-skull')], cert='C1', parts=['dog_teeth', 'dog_mandible', 'dog_turb_olf'])
B('dog_mandible', 'Lower jaw (mandible)', .15, 'The lower jaw is a single hinge of bone that opens and closes to bite and carry.',
  'The two halves of the lower jaw are joined at the chin. They swing on a hinge joint in front of the ear. A dog\'s jaw moves mainly up and down like a pair of scissors, with little sideways grinding.',
  'The jaw is the same bone as the human mandible; the dog\'s is long and narrow and carries 22 teeth, with a tall flange (the coronoid process) where the strong temporalis muscle attaches.',
  'The model draws the jaw as a rod and a ramus on each side, a simplified outline.', [['Lower teeth', '22 (I3 C1 P4 M3 on each side)']], [('mandible', 'In people: the mandible; same bone, shorter, with a chin.')], refs=[A('7.2', '7-2-the-skull')], ext=[WDS, DENT])
B('dog_teeth', 'Teeth (42)', .02, 'An adult dog has 42 teeth: for each side, 3 incisors, 1 canine, 4 premolars and 2 molars above, and the same with 3 molars below.',
  'Dogs use their teeth to bite, cut, carry and tear, not to chew much. The long pointed teeth at the front corners are the canines; the small ones at the front are the incisors; the big sharp ones at the back of the mouth cut like scissors.',
  'The dental formula is 2 x (incisors 3/3, canines 1/1, premolars 4/4, molars 2/3) = 42. The carnassial teeth, the upper fourth premolar and the lower first molar, shear against each other like scissors to slice meat. People have 32 adult teeth with flat molars for grinding.',
  'Teeth are made of enamel over dentine around a pulp cavity. Dogs are born without visible teeth and get a set of baby teeth, then replace them with the adult set. The number of molars differs: upper 2, lower 3 per side.',
  [['Dental formula', '2 x (I3/3 C1/1 P4/4 M2/3) = 42'], ['Human adult teeth', '32']], [('teeth', 'In people: 32 adult teeth (8 incisors, 4 canines, 8 premolars, 12 molars); the same types, fewer, with flatter molars.')], ext=[DENT, WDA], refs=[R('34.1', '34-1-digestive-systems')])
bars('dog_teeth', [('dog: adult teeth', 42), ('human: adult teeth', 32)], 'number of teeth', 'Adult teeth: a dog has 42 (2 x (I3/3, C1/1, P4/4, M2/3), dvm360); people have 32.')
B('dog_cervical', 'Neck vertebrae (7)', .22, 'Seven neck vertebrae, the same number as in people and giraffes, make a flexible neck.',
  'Almost every mammal has seven neck vertebrae. The first two are special: the atlas lets the head nod and the axis lets it turn.',
  'In a dog the neck vertebrae are long and wide, so the neck is strong and mobile for carrying the head and the sniffing nose. The first (the atlas) has large wing-like sides; the second (the axis) has a long peg (the dens) that lets the head turn.',
  'The model draws every vertebra separately along the neck line (7), each with a body, a spinous process and side processes; fine joints and discs are omitted.',
  [['Number', '7 (C1-C7)'], ['Human', '7']], [('cervical_spine', 'In people: also 7, the same bones. Differences: the dog\'s neck is held forward and angled, ours is upright.')], refs=[A('7.3', '7-3-the-vertebral-column')], ext=[VERT, WDS])
B('dog_thoracic', 'Chest vertebrae (13)', .27, 'Thirteen vertebrae in the chest each carry a pair of ribs; people have twelve.',
  'The chest vertebrae form the middle of the back and connect to the ribs. In a dog the first ones have tall spikes that rise above the shoulders; the withers are at the top of these spikes.',
  'The thoracic vertebrae are alike from dog to dog (13), and tall spinous processes at the front give the muscles of the shoulders and neck something to pull on. People have 12 and an upright back.',
  'The spikes lean backward at the front of the series and forward near the back, with an "anticlinal" vertebra in between where they point straight up; the model draws the lean approximately.',
  [['Number', '13 (T1-T13)'], ['Human', '12']], [('thoracic_spine', 'In people: 12 thoracic vertebrae; the same kind of vertebra, one fewer.')], refs=[A('7.3', '7-3-the-vertebral-column')], ext=[VERT])
B('dog_lumbar', 'Lower-back vertebrae (7)', .24, 'Seven lumbar vertebrae in the loin let the dog arch and stretch its back when it runs.',
  'The lower back has no ribs. Its vertebrae are large and flex at a gallop, so the back works like a spring.',
  'A dog has 7 lumbar vertebrae and a person 5. The long side wings (transverse processes) point forward and downward and carry the big back muscles.',
  'About 4.5% of dogs in one study of 89 had a transitional vertebra between the lumbar and sacral parts.', [['Number', '7 (L1-L7)'], ['Human', '5']], [('lumbar_spine', 'In people: 5 lumbar vertebrae, which bear the weight of an upright body; the dog\'s carry only the horizontal body.')], refs=[A('7.3', '7-3-the-vertebral-column')], ext=[VERT])
B('dog_sacrum', 'Sacrum (3 fused)', .08, 'Three vertebrae fused into one plate join the spine to the pelvis.',
  'The sacrum is the strong link between the backbone and the hip bones. It is firm because the weight of the hindquarters pushes through it.',
  'A dog\'s sacrum has 3 fused vertebrae, a person\'s has 5.', 'The sacrum joins the hip bones at the sacroiliac joints, which move very little.', [['Number', '3 fused'], ['Human', '5 fused']], [('sacrum', 'In people: 5 fused sacral vertebrae.')], refs=[A('7.3', '7-3-the-vertebral-column')], ext=[VERT])
B('dog_caudal', 'Tail vertebrae (about 20)', .3, 'About twenty small vertebrae make the tail, from big blocks at the base to slender rods at the tip.',
  'The tail balances the dog when it turns, helps it swim as a rudder, and signals mood. A Labrador\'s "otter" tail is thick at the base and tapers.',
  'Dogs have 19-23 tail vertebrae depending on breed and tail length (about 20 here); people have a tiny tailbone of 3-5 fused vertebrae, the coccyx. The tail is moved by muscles along its sides.',
  'Some breeds are born without a tail or with a short one; wagging is done by muscles attached to the first tail bones.', [['Number', '≈ 20 (varies 19-23)'], ['Human', '3-5 fused (the coccyx)']], [('pelvis', 'In people the tailbone (coccyx) is the equivalent, a few small fused bones inside the pelvis.')], ext=[VERT, UKC], refs=[A('7.3', '7-3-the-vertebral-column')])
B('dog_ribs', 'Ribs (13 pairs)', .2, 'Thirteen pairs of ribs form the dog\'s deep, narrow chest: nine join the breastbone, four do not.',
  'The ribs protect the heart and lungs and move with each breath. A dog\'s chest is deep from top to bottom and narrow side to side, which suits running.',
  'Nine pairs of ribs link to the breastbone by cartilage (the true ribs), four do not (the false and floating ribs); the last is usually free. People have 12 pairs, with 7 reaching the sternum directly.',
  'The model draws the 13 pairs with their cartilage tips; their spacing and curve are typical, not measured.', [['Pairs', '13 (9 sternal + 4 asternal)'], ['Human', '12 pairs']], [('ribcage', 'In people: 12 pairs, a barrel-shaped chest that is wide from side to side.')],
  refs=[A('7.4', '7-4-the-thoracic-cage')], ext=[src('Dog ribs (vetscraft)', 'https://vetscraft.com/ribs'), WDS], cert='C2', parts=['dog_costal', 'dog_sternum'])
B('dog_costal', 'Costal cartilages', .08, 'Bars of cartilage join the ribs to the breastbone and let the chest flex.', 'Each rib continues in cartilage, which is more springy than bone, so the chest expands for breathing and gives under a knock.',
  'In a dog the costal cartilages are long in the back ribs (the last true ribs and the arch), which curve forward and join the cartilage in front.', 'Costal cartilage is hyaline cartilage and often hardens (calcifies) with age.', [['Material', 'hyaline cartilage']], [('ribcage', 'In people: the same, but shorter and more uniformly spaced.')], ext=[WDS], refs=[A('7.4', '7-4-the-thoracic-cage')], cert='C2')
B('dog_sternum', 'Breastbone (sternum)', .22, 'The sternum is a row of eight small bones along the chest floor that the ribs hang from.',
  'The dog\'s sternum is a keel at the bottom of the chest. The front end projects between the front legs; the last bone ends in a flexible tip of cartilage.',
  'The dog\'s sternum is made of a series of segments (sternebrae), usually eight, instead of the three fused parts in people (manubrium, body, xiphoid process).',
  'The model draws eight segments and a cartilage tip, approximate in length.', [['Segments', '8 (sternebrae)'], ['Human', '3 fused parts']], [('sternum', 'In people: three fused parts; the same function.')], refs=[A('7.4', '7-4-the-thoracic-cage')], cert='C2')
B('dog_pelvis', 'Pelvis', .2, 'The pelvis is the strong bony ring the hind legs swing from.',
  'Two hip bones join the spine (through the sacrum) and meet at the bottom. The hip sockets face sideways, so the thigh swings back and forth under the body.',
  'Each hip bone is three bones fused: the ilium (a blade sloping up toward the spine), the ischium (the point you can feel at the rump) and the pubis. In people the ilium is a broad bowl; in dogs it is long and narrow.',
  'The model\'s pelvis is a smoothed shape, not a bone-by-bone reconstruction.', [['Hip bones', 'ilium, ischium, pubis (fused)']], [('pelvis', 'In people: the same three bones forming a bowl. Different: the dog\'s pelvis is long and tilted for a horizontal back.')], refs=[A('8.3', '8-3-the-pelvic-girdle-and-pelvis')], cert='C2')
B('dog_baculum', 'Penis bone (baculum)', .1, 'Male dogs have a bone in the penis, the os penis, that stiffens it.',
  'The model is a male, so it has this bone. It lies inside the penis, beneath the belly, and has a groove for the urethra.', 'Many mammals have a baculum; people do not. In dogs it sits in the penis alongside an erectile part that swells.',
  'The model\'s shape and position are schematic: the bone is placed under the abdomen, near where it would lie in the sheath.', [['Present in', 'male dogs and many other mammals'], ['Human', 'absent']], [('long_bone', 'No counterpart in people.')], ext=[WBAC], cert='C2')
SIDE = lambda s: 'left' if s == 'l' else 'right'
B('dog_scapula', 'Shoulder blade (scapula)', .17, 'The shoulder blade lies flat on the side of the chest and is held on by muscle only.',
  'A dog has no collarbone to brace the shoulder, so the shoulder blade is joined to the body only by muscles. It can swing, which lengthens the stride.',
  'Same bone as in people. A ridge (the spine of the scapula) runs along it and has the shoulder joint at the lower end. In people the scapula is on the back and braced by the collarbone; in the dog it stands on the side of the chest.',
  'The model draws a flat blade with a ridge. Dogs have a tiny collarbone remnant embedded in a muscle; it is not drawn.', [['Collarbone', 'no working clavicle']], [('scapula_l', 'In people: the same bone, on the back of the chest, braced by the collarbone (clavicle_l).')], refs=[A('8.1', '8-1-the-pectoral-girdle')], ext=[WDA, WDS], cert='C1')
B('dog_humerus', 'Upper arm bone (humerus)', .17, 'The dog\'s upper arm bone runs from the shoulder back to the elbow, tucked close to the chest.',
  'The humerus is the same bone as in your upper arm. In a dog it slopes back, so the elbow sits just behind the chest, level with the lowest point of the ribs.',
  'In people the humerus hangs down from a shoulder on the side; in the dog it is angled so the elbow is behind and the foot is below the body.', 'The model draws a shaft with rounded ends, slightly curved.', [['Counterpart', 'human humerus']], [('humerus_l', 'In people: the same bone.')], refs=[A('8.2', '8-2-bones-of-the-upper-limb')])
B('dog_radius_ulna', 'Forearm bones (radius and ulna)', .19, 'Two long bones make the forearm, and the ulna sticks out behind the elbow as a point.',
  'The point of the elbow is the end of the ulna, the same bone you can feel behind your own elbow. The radius carries most of the weight in a dog.',
  'The radius and ulna are fixed together so the dog\'s forearm twists very little, unlike yours; the two bones lock into one stiff post for running.', 'The model draws the two as separate rods with an elbow point.', [['Counterpart', 'human radius and ulna']], [('radius_ulna_l', 'In people: the same two bones; people can rotate the forearm (palm up or down), dogs very little.')], refs=[A('8.2', '8-2-bones-of-the-upper-limb')])
B('dog_carpus', 'Carpus (the dog\'s wrist)', .05, 'The "knee" on a dog\'s front leg is its wrist, the carpus: a cluster of small bones halfway down the leg.',
  'People call it the front "knee", but it is the same joint as your wrist. The dog walks on its fingertips, so the wrist is far from the ground.',
  'The carpus has seven bones in two rows, like your eight. A dog stands on the toes, not the palm, which is why the wrist is high (digitigrade stance).', 'The model draws seven small bones as balls; their real shapes are more irregular.', [['Bones', '7']], [('hand_bones_l', 'In people: the wrist has 8 bones, and the palm rests on the ground only when crawling.')], refs=[A('8.2', '8-2-bones-of-the-upper-limb')], cert='C2')
B('dog_metacarpals', 'Metacarpals (palm bones)', .07, 'Four long palm bones stand almost upright, so the dog stands on its toes.',
  'In you the palm bones are in the hand. In the dog they are the long straight bones in the lower foreleg, between wrist and toes.', 'The first metacarpal (the thumb side) is short and goes with the dewclaw, the other four reach the ground.', 'The model draws four.', [['Weight-bearing', '4 (digits II-V)']], [('hand_bones_l', 'In people: five metacarpals in the palm.')], refs=[A('8.2', '8-2-bones-of-the-upper-limb')])
B('dog_phalanges', 'Toe bones', .05, 'Each toe has three small bones, like your fingers, and the dog stands on them.',
  'The toes are what a dog\'s foot is made of: bones, pads and claws. The foot is arched so the weight falls on the pads.', 'Three phalanges per toe (the last carries the claw). A forefoot has four weight-bearing toes plus the dewclaw; a hind foot has four.', 'The model draws three bones per toe.', [['Phalanges', '3 per toe']], [('hand_bones_l', 'In people: the fingers have 3 bones, the thumb 2.'), ('foot_bones_l', 'In people the toes also have 3 bones (the big toe 2).')], refs=[A('8.2', '8-2-bones-of-the-upper-limb')])
B('dog_dewclaw', 'Dewclaw (first toe)', .03, 'The dewclaw is the dog\'s thumb: a small toe on the inner side of the foreleg that often does not touch the ground.',
  'It is the first toe, your thumb counterpart. It is higher up the leg than the other toes and has a claw.', 'Forelegs carry a dewclaw; hind dewclaws are rare in a Labrador. Some are attached only by skin and are often removed in some breeds.', 'The model draws a two-bone toe with a claw.', [['Digit', 'first (I)']], [('hand_bones_l', 'In people: the thumb.')], refs=[A('8.2', '8-2-bones-of-the-upper-limb')], cert='C2')
B('dog_femur', 'Thigh bone (femur)', .2, 'The femur is the long bone of the thigh, sloping forward from the hip to the knee.',
  'The femur is the same bone as yours. In a dog it slopes forward and down from the hip to the stifle (the dog\'s knee), which is at the front of the flank.', 'Same joint: a ball at the top fits into the hip socket. The dog\'s femur is more angled.', 'The model draws a curved shaft with rounded ends.', [['Counterpart', 'human femur']], [('femur_l', 'In people: the same bone; ours stands upright.')], refs=[A('8.4', '8-4-bones-of-the-lower-limb')])
B('dog_patella', 'Kneecap (patella)', .025, 'The kneecap sits in front of the knee (the stifle) in the tendon of the big thigh muscle.', 'It acts like a pulley: the front thigh muscle pulls on it and it straightens the leg.', 'The patella is the same bone as yours. In small dogs it can slip out of its groove.', 'The model draws a small oval bone.', [['Counterpart', 'human patella']], [('patella_l', 'In people: the same bone.')], refs=[A('8.4', '8-4-bones-of-the-lower-limb')])
B('dog_tibia', 'Shin bones (tibia and fibula)', .2, 'The tibia carries the weight below the stifle; the thin fibula runs beside it.', 'The shin runs from the stifle down and back to the hock. The thin bone alongside is the fibula.', 'The same pair of bones as in your lower leg.', 'The model draws the two as rods.', [['Counterpart', 'human tibia and fibula']], [('tibia_fibula_l', 'In people: the same bones.')], refs=[A('8.4', '8-4-bones-of-the-lower-limb')])
B('dog_tarsus', 'Tarsus (the hock and heel bone)', .05, 'The hock is the dog\'s ankle: the pointed bone sticking up and back is the calcaneus, the heel bone.',
  'The sharp-angled joint halfway up a dog\'s hind leg looks like a backward knee, but it is the ankle. The pointed bone at its back is your heel bone. The dog walks on its toes, so the heel is high above the ground.',
  'The tarsus is made of seven bones like yours. The calcaneus is long and forms a lever for the calf muscle (via the calcaneal tendon, the dog\'s Achilles).', 'The model draws the heel bone (calcaneus), the talus and four other tarsal bones as simple shapes.', [['Bones', '7']], [('foot_bones_l', 'In people: the same bones, but the heel rests on the ground.')], refs=[A('8.4', '8-4-bones-of-the-lower-limb')], cert='C2')
B('dog_metatarsals', 'Metatarsals (sole bones)', .1, 'Long sole bones stand upright in the lower hind leg.', 'In you these are the bones of the middle of the foot. In a dog they form the long lower part of the hind leg, from hock to toes.', 'Four long bones for the four hind toes.', 'The model draws four.', [['Weight-bearing', '4']], [('foot_bones_l', 'In people: five metatarsals.')], refs=[A('8.4', '8-4-bones-of-the-lower-limb')])
